# Project structure

## Purpose

Keep every workspace identical in shape so agents, scripts, and schemas can
find any artifact by convention instead of by search.

## Scope

All layers, all tasks. Governs where files are created inside a workspace,
which schema each structured file must satisfy, and where one workspace ends
and the next begins.

## Core Principles

- One canonical layout, defined in `docs/conventions.md`. Agents MUST create
  files only in their designated locations and NEVER invent sibling
  directories or alternate spellings.
- Every structured file is YAML validated against a schema in `schemas/`.
  Human-readable documents are Markdown.
- Directory placement encodes meaning: the path of an artifact states what it
  is and which issue and page it belongs to.
- A workspace is self-contained. Nothing in it points outside its own root.

## Workspace roots

- A workspace root is any directory that contains `project.yaml`. The name
  `production/` is the default that `scripts/create-project.js` uses. It is
  not a requirement.
- Scripts and hooks resolve the root as the nearest ancestor directory holding
  `project.yaml`, or take it from `--root`. They never look for a directory
  called `production`.
- Parallel productions in one clone live side by side (`production/`,
  `production-<slug>/`), each with its own bibles, references, and continuity.
- Assets, references, and continuity NEVER cross roots. A path stored in a
  workspace file is relative to that workspace's root and MUST NOT climb out
  of it. A reference image that two productions need is copied into each one
  and registered in each `references/manifest.yaml`.

## Layout

```
production/
├── project.yaml               # project.schema.json
├── story/                     # source/ (source text), story-bible.yaml, series-plan.yaml
├── style/                     # style-bible.md, style-block.txt (frozen prompt block), color-script.md
├── characters/                # <CHAR_ID>/character.yaml, wardrobe.yaml, refs/ (sheets, prompts/)
├── locations/                 # <LOC_ID>/location.yaml, map.yaml, refs/ (plates, prompts/)
├── props/                     # <PROP_ID>/prop.yaml, refs/ (sheets, prompts/)
├── references/                # manifest.yaml (registry of ALL reference material), inbox/, style/ (look pages, prompts/), docs/
├── issues/
│   └── <ISS_ID>/
│       ├── issue.yaml         # issue.schema.json
│       ├── scenes/            # <SC_ID>.yaml (scene.schema.json)
│       └── pages/
│           └── <PG_ID>/
│               ├── page.yaml              # page.schema.json: layout, panels, ledger, lettering text
│               ├── lettering.yaml         # lettering.schema.json (overlay mode only)
│               ├── reference-plan.yaml    # reference-plan.schema.json
│               ├── thumbnails/            # rough layouts
│               ├── prompts/               # <provider>.yaml, <provider>.<PN_ID>.yaml (prompt-package.schema.json)
│               ├── renders/               # <PG_ID>_V##.png|jpg versioned candidates, never overwritten
│               └── reviews/               # <RENDER_ID>.review.yaml (visual-review.schema.json)
├── generations/               # <PG_ID or ENTITY_ID>/GEN_*.yaml (generation-record.schema.json), GEN_*.request.json
├── continuity/                # <ISS_ID>.final-state.yaml (continuity-state.schema.json)
└── editions/
    └── <ED_ID>/               # edition.yaml (edition.schema.json), exports/
```

## Placement rules

- Entity directories are named by ID: `characters/CHAR_MARA/`,
  `locations/LOC_FERRY_DECK/`, `props/PROP_FLARE_GUN/`. The ID in the path
  MUST match the `id` field inside the file.
- Source text is stored unmodified under `story/source/`. Analysis and
  adaptation write to the story bible and the series plan, never into the
  source file.
- Reference images for an entity live in that entity's `refs/`. Look assets
  live in `references/style/`. Every one of them, wherever it sits, is listed
  in `references/manifest.yaml` with its path.
- `references/inbox/` is the drop zone for user-supplied material waiting for
  `/ingest-references`. Nothing in the inbox is attached to a request.
- Everything that belongs to one page stays in that page's directory: plan,
  lettering geometry, reference plan, thumbnails, prompts, renders, and
  reviews. A review file sits in `reviews/` and is named after the render it
  judges.
- Generation records live under `generations/`, in a directory named after
  the page or the entity they were made for. Each record has its request
  file, `GEN_*.request.json`, beside it.
- The prompt package of a reference sheet sits in a `prompts/` directory
  inside the entity's `refs/`, and a look page's in
  `references/style/prompts/`. `compile-prompts.js --asset` writes them
  there.
- One continuity file per issue, written at the end of the issue. Per-panel
  state lives in the ledger rows of `page.yaml`.
- Each edition has its own directory. Exports go in its `exports/` and are
  never written over (see `publishing/edition-assembly.md`).
- Agents MUST NOT write outside the workspace root except when editing the
  scaffold itself, and NEVER write into the generated harness directories.
- Deleting or moving an approved asset is forbidden. Supersede it (see
  `approval-policy.md`).

## Validation

- `scripts/validate-workspace.js` finds the root by the `project.yaml`
  marker, validates every structured file against its schema
  (`schemas/<name>.schema.json`), and resolves cross-references inside that
  root only.
- The scripts read fixed paths. A page, review, or record saved in the wrong
  place is reported as missing or as an unresolved reference.
- `hooks/validate-after-write.js` validates schema-known artifacts when they
  are written.
- Whether a stored path climbs out of the root, and whether a file was copied
  between workspaces with its registry entry, is checked by `production-qa`
  at phase gates.
- Structural exceptions require human review and a conventions update first.
